The review owed was performed. Its closure cannot be accepted while the new brake can be suppressed by a contributor's untrusted branch name. This is a new finding against T, not a claim that the five earlier fixes failed.

Finding F1 (P1): warrant/gate.py:1123-1127 collects the branch name of every assessment and skips a braked merge whenever any name starts with gate/revert/<PR>-. survey() (1290-1311) includes refused assessments. facts() (1276-1281) obtains branch from head.ref, including forks. No repository, author, exact merge identity, or actual revert check is made before that branch name suppresses enforcement.

A contributor opens a fork PR from gate/revert/7-blocker with an unrelated, real code.py diff. It is refused with REVERT_NOT_EXACT (its author is mallory, not github-actions[bot]), but its branch still suppresses the legitimate revert of vetoed PR #7. No privileged credentials, trusted record, acceptance or successful CI is required for this suppression; the probe supplies green checks to isolate the flaw. With that PR left open, each gate run skips the brake. Once its deadline expires it is no longer enforced. Thus the owner cannot rely on hold or a challenge to cause the promised revert.

The fixture has one genuinely open braked merge that weakened AGENTS.md. A mocked GitHub pull read reports the owner's hold. Without the attacker assessment the real open_revert code makes tree/commit/ref/PR creation calls and dispatches CI. With it, enforce_brakes returns [] and makes no API calls. All GitHub writes in this experiment are in-memory stub calls; no external PR was opened. The probe fails on its F1 assertion at both 58e3cc74e3d2611d73a4917409394ae7117f3630 and M. A diagnostic guard confined to an extracted copy (require repository identity and bot PR author before treating a branch as pending revert) makes this case pass. That diagnostic is not a proposed complete fix: a real fix should bind suppression to the exact braked merge and an authenticated gate-created revert, while retaining legitimate reverts awaiting CI.

Required follow-up: an ordinary constitutional PR fixing this bypass, with regression test and a mutant that removes the identity/merge binding; keep CI green. No repository code was edited in this verdict worktree.

